1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:05,284
这么冷的清晨，河水却没有结冰，还升腾着阵阵白汽。
On a morning this cold, the river still hasn’t frozen; it breathes white steam.

2
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:07,570
这里是北海道东部的湿地。
This is a wetland in eastern Hokkaido.

3
00:00:11,026 --> 00:00:14,250
站在河水里安静睡觉的，是丹顶鹤。
Asleep, standing in the water: red-crowned cranes.

4
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:24,500
早年间，随着湿地被一片片开垦，它们差点就彻底失去了家园。
Long ago, as the wetlands were cleared field by field, they nearly lost their home for good.

5
00:00:24,550 --> 00:00:29,100
天亮后，它们成群飞去的地方，是一块专门撒满玉米的田地。
At daybreak they fly in flocks to a field scattered with corn, just for them.

6
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:34,100
回想一九五二年冬天，整个北海道，一共只找到了三十三只丹顶鹤。
Think back to the winter of 1952: in all of Hokkaido, only 33 cranes were found.

7
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:38,400
也是从那一年起，当地人决定在冬天给它们投喂食物。
That was the year local people decided to feed them through the winter.

8
00:00:38,450 --> 00:00:41,782
就这样，丹顶鹤的数量，一年比一年多。
And so, year after year, there were more of them.

9
00:00:41,850 --> 00:00:45,498
到了二零二五年，已经数到了一千九百二十七只。
By 2025 the count had reached 1,927.

10
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:49,829
它们的保护级别，也终于从易危，降到了近危。
Their status finally eased, from Vulnerable to Near Threatened.

11
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:57,038
虽然不再算作濒危物种了，但这群雪地里的精灵，依然需要人们的守护。
No longer counted as endangered, these spirits of the snow still need people to look after them.
